# RKE2 server CA for the on-prem cluster a1-ops-prd (Rancher cluster id c-jdfbj).
#
# This is the TRUST ANCHOR for every deploy job that reaches the on-prem cluster
# from a runner outside it. It is a PUBLIC certificate, not a secret — it is
# committed rather than held in a repo variable so that changing what CI trusts
# requires a reviewed diff instead of a silent settings edit.
#
# Runners inside a1-ops-prd used the projected ServiceAccount CA at
# /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt. That path exists on a DFW
# runner too and contains the DFW cluster's CA, so relying on it after the
# relocation silently trusts the wrong cluster. Hence this explicit file.
#
#   subject/issuer : CN=rke2-server-ca@1748493655
#   notBefore      : 2025-05-29
#   notAfter       : 2035-05-27
#   server         : https://a1-ops-prd.support.tools:6443
#                    (the DNS name, NOT the raw IP — 96.90.99.201 is a
#                     residential address that can change. The name is in
#                     this certificate's SANs, so TLS validates either way,
#                     and DNS absorbs an IP change instead of 9 repos.)
#
# Regenerate with:
#   kubectl config view --raw -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.certificate-authority-data}' | base64 -d
